finish('exact-semidefinite-feasibility-polynomial-turing-time',
 id='TCS-6574', title='Is exact semidefinite feasibility in polynomial time?',
 area='Optimization and numerics', criterion='resources', question_type='yes_no',
 formal='Is the following language in P in the ordinary Turing model: all lists of symmetric rational matrices A_0,A_1,…,A_m∈Q^{n×n} for which there exists a real vector x∈R^m satisfying A_0+sum_{i=1}^m x_i A_i⪰0? Both m and n are unbounded parts of the input, and the algorithm must decide exact nonemptiness in time polynomial in the total binary encoding length.',
 definitions='Every matrix entry is an explicitly supplied rational number, with signed binary numerator and positive binary denominator; all matrices have the same size n≥1. A symmetric matrix M is positive semidefinite, written M⪰0, when zᵀMz≥0 for every real vector z, equivalently when all eigenvalues are nonnegative. The unknown x ranges over all real vectors, not just rational vectors. Singular feasible matrices count as YES. There is no strict-feasibility, boundedness, conditioning, rank, or distance-to-infeasibility promise, and no radius or accuracy parameter is supplied. The output is one bit specifying whether any feasible x exists; an explicit feasible point is not required. An affine subspace given by rational linear equations can be parametrized by rational Gaussian elimination, so this is also the rational-affine-subspace intersection formulation.',
 answer_criterion='Give a deterministic polynomial-time bit algorithm deciding every instance, or prove that this language is outside P. Producing approximately positive semidefinite matrices, deciding only a promised well-conditioned case, or showing that explicit feasible points can have very long encodings does not settle this decision problem.',
 context=[
  paragraph('Semidefinite constraints replace scalar nonnegativity by nonnegativity of a quadratic form in every direction. They include linear inequalities as the diagonal-matrix special case and support powerful relaxations in combinatorial optimization. Approximation algorithms are well developed, but their guarantees require attention to input geometry and numerical precision. De Klerk and Vallentin establish polynomial bit complexity for a short-step interior-point method under appropriate assumptions; their theorem is about controlled approximation, not arbitrary exact feasibility.','interior'),
  paragraph('The unrestricted problem is decidable. Introduce a real n×n matrix Y and impose A_0+sum_i x_i A_i=YYᵀ. These are finitely many polynomial equations of degree at most two, and a real symmetric matrix is positive semidefinite exactly when such a factor exists. General real-algebraic decision procedures therefore apply. Henrion, Naldi and Safey El Din develop more specialized symbolic algorithms, including treatment of degenerate feasible sets. None of this supplies polynomial time when both input dimensions vary.','symbolic'),
  paragraph('Exact feasibility can differ even from arbitrarily close approximation. The matrix M(t) with rows (0,1) and (1,t) has determinant −1, so it is never positive semidefinite. Yet as t tends to positive infinity, its negative eigenvalue (t−√(t²+4))/2 tends to zero. Thus these infeasible matrices approach the positive semidefinite cone arbitrarily closely. A numerical tolerance alone cannot distinguish this situation from a singular feasible matrix.'),
  paragraph('Rational input also need not admit a rational witness. Consider a block diagonal matrix with blocks [[x,2],[2,2x]] and [[2,x],[x,1]]. The first block is positive semidefinite exactly when x≥√2, and the second when |x|≤√2. The whole matrix is therefore feasible exactly at x=√2. All coefficient matrices are rational, but searching only rational substitutions would miss the solution. This example explains why the domain of the unknowns is part of the formal question.'),
  paragraph('Even rational feasible points can be enormous. Conditions x_1≥2 and [[x_{i+1},x_i],[x_i,1]]⪰0 force x_{i+1}≥x_i², hence x_k≥2^{2^{k−1}}. A block diagonal LMI encodes the chain compactly, while writing its final coordinate in ordinary binary needs exponentially many digits in k. Pataki and Touzov analyze how such growth arises beyond isolated examples, including connections to singularity degree. Large explicit witnesses obstruct a naive search algorithm; they do not prove that deciding existence is hard.','large'),
  paragraph('Ramana’s exact dual provides an alternative feasibility system of polynomial description size without regularity assumptions. In the Turing model, it implies that semidefinite feasibility belongs to NP if and only if it belongs to coNP. It does not establish either membership individually. The corresponding NP∩coNP theorem over exact real-number machines belongs to a different computational model. This distinction prevents a common overstatement of what SDP duality proves about bit complexity.','ramana'),
  paragraph('Pataki’s October 2025 work gives a new derivation and a fuller description of the feasible set of Ramana’s dual using facial reduction.','newdual'),
  paragraph('A separate STOC 2026 result by Slot, Steurer and Wiedmer obtains small approximate solutions for minimizing convex polynomials over polyhedra. That result concerns polynomial objectives with linear constraints; the authors explicitly contrast it with the large-solution obstacle for semidefinite programs. It does not settle general exact semidefinite feasibility.','hesse')],
 why='A basic boundary between numerical convex optimization and exact efficient computation. Resolving it would clarify what semidefinite programming can guarantee without hidden regularity or witness-size assumptions, with consequences for optimization relaxations, algebraic certificates and verification.',
 importance=dict(score=97, method='editorial', assessed_on='2026-09-10', reason='A foundational complexity gap in a widely used convex optimization model; exact decision remains open despite powerful approximation algorithms, exact duality and symbolic decidability.'),
 progress=[
  step('1997','Ramana constructs an exact dual without regularity assumptions and proves simultaneous NP/coNP membership in the Turing model, without proving membership in either class.','ramana'),
  step('2016-09-21','De Klerk and Vallentin establish Turing-model polynomial approximation complexity for a short-step interior-point method under their stated assumptions.','interior'),
  step('2018–2021','Henrion, Naldi and Safey El Din develop symbolic homotopy for degenerate feasible sets. Generic auxiliary choices and objectives remain in their algorithmic analysis; fixed-parameter arithmetic bounds are not a general polynomial bit bound.','symbolic'),
  step('2024-03-08','Pataki and Touzov publish a structural analysis of exponentially large feasible solutions and partial approaches to representing them compactly.','large'),
  step('2025-10-08','Pataki’s preprint gives a combinatorial treatment of Ramana’s exact dual and its feasible set, preserving the unresolved Turing-model complexity distinction.','newdual'),
  step('2026 — STOC','Slot, Steurer and Wiedmer prove polynomial bit-length bounds for approximate optima of convex polynomial minimization over polyhedra. The result does not cover exact feasibility of arbitrary linear matrix inequalities.','hesse')],
 references=[
  ref('ramana','An exact duality theory for semidefinite programming and its complexity implications','Motakuri Venkata Ramana',1997,'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02614433','Mathematical Programming 77:129–162; exact alternatives and distinct Turing/real-machine complexity conclusions'),
  ref('interior','On the Turing Model Complexity of Interior Point Methods for Semidefinite Programming','Etienne de Klerk; Frank Vallentin',2016,'https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/15M103114X','SIAM Journal on Optimization 26(3):1944–1961; approximation with controlled intermediate bit lengths, under assumptions'),
  ref('symbolic','Exact algorithms for semidefinite programs with degenerate feasible set','Didier Henrion; Simone Naldi; Mohab Safey El Din',2021,'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0747717120301176','Journal of Symbolic Computation 104:942–959; DOI 10.1016/j.jsc.2020.11.001; ISSAC 2018 precursor/arXiv:1802.02834; §1.2 generic objectives and arithmetic-complexity qualifications'),
  ref('large','How Do Exponential Size Solutions Arise in Semidefinite Programming?','Gábor Pataki; Aleksandr Touzov',2024,'https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/21M1434945','SIAM Journal on Optimization 34(1), published 8 March 2024; full preprint arXiv:2103.00041v2, 26 July 2023'),
  ref('newdual','A combinatorial approach to Ramana’s exact dual for semidefinite programming','Gábor Pataki',2025,'https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07271','Preprint v1, 8 October 2025; §1.2 and §2.6 exact alternative systems'),
  ref('hesse','Hesse’s Redemption: Efficient Convex Polynomial Programming','Lucas Slot; David Steurer; Manuel Wiedmer',2026,'https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.03440','STOC 2026, 410–419; DOI 10.1145/3798129.3800760; preprint v1, 5 November 2025; approximate minimization over a polyhedron, with explicit contrast to SDP')],
 status='source_open', status_note='Checked through 10 September 2026. The primary literature retains general exact SDP feasibility as an open polynomial-time problem. Ramana’s dual does not establish NP∩coNP membership in the Turing model, and recent convex-polynomial optimization results address a different constraint family. This card permits real witnesses, singular feasible points, weak infeasibility and unbounded feasible regions.', formulation_reviewed_on='2026-09-10')
